nity  now on the other side of the scale the X  cubed term will dominate for negative  values of X but this term is negative  and that's negative so up here  it will go off to infinity in the  positive direction now where are the  zeros of the potential function there's  one obviously one at x equals zero and  with these values it's not a hundred  percent obvious immediately but 4x  squared minus 2 B X cubed if you set X  equal to 2 4 times 2 is 8 B 2 times 2 4  times 4 is 16 if B is 2 and X is 2  that's 16 so we get a cancellation at x  equals 2 and so we have a point up here  I'm sorry that's our zero point at x  equals 2 now we said that the force at x  equals 4/3 had a zero and so if we plot  this function it's gonna look something  like that  now let's focus on these two points  right here where our force is zero  remember that this is minus the slope  and so we can see immediately that these  two points have zero slope and so the  force is zero there notice potential  energy always depends on a reference  point so whether the potential energy is  zero not is not crucial but the physical  fact that the force is zero there has  physical meaning so here this is one of  our points X X F F X 0 now what is the  property of this point well suppose we  ask ourselves if the particle were just  displaced slightly from this position  where the force is 0 which way with the  force point well in order to answer a  question like that we can look at the  slopes on either side of the zero so  over here the slope is negative but the  force is minus the slope so f of X is  positive on this side and so if a  particle is displaced a little bit from  the zero point it moves off to the right  and it will continue to feel a force in  this direction and so it will move away  from this zero point of the force now on  the other side we do the same type of  argument and over here the slope is  positive but the force is negative so  again what we see on this side the  particle will move 